    consume("%d %lf", n, d) with the doubleword vmov r2,r3,d0, the base
    convention a variadic callee takes. That instruction reads d0 whole, but
    heritage refines d0 into s0 and s1, and the call reads them as its r2 and
    r3 arguments, the IR two floats moved one by one also give. Before, show2
    took s0, s1 and r0 as three unsigned words. The move, noted from the raw
    p-code, makes the words one double; the call keeps passing its two words.
    The words print as the double's bits, low then high, as C that compiles.
    show_d1 moves d1 below an unused float slot, show3 moves d0 beside a d1 it
    stores for the call, pun hands d0's bits to a 64-bit integer in r0:r1.
    Only the double each takes is asserted: show_d1's and show3's other
    parameters, pun's return and the r1 the calls forward are as on main.
    two_f moves two floats with vmov r0,s0 and
    vmov r1,s1 and keeps them as two words, and show_f converts its float into
    d7 and is unchanged. The caller that forwards show2's inputs needs
    show2's stated prototype, so a callee-first decompile-all checks it
    (kuna-cli/tests/arm_float_arguments.rs).

    A last pass with armfloatargs off keeps main's three words (#9).
    Asserts #1 to #4, #7 and #9 fail on main (main gives three words in
    every pass); #8 guards the printing of the words; #5 and #6 are controls.
